uid "dfe6c093-26dc-5d7a-ada3-01470001afa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186268153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186266666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268818 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ebf581fc-936a-577e-8513-af09419c38fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e48008ac-4acd-5d33-8b94-d523fa814086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269230 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce6f50bf-ae89-57dc-a707-1d28279d986e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269242 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269243 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=9,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0157c09c-4c34-5a09-a678-edce758d03a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269243 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={12}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={12}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269244 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d4/d4947e37239d2ac2950b536d7afc2d0810ea713c437b7095a43e123d50ff2b34.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f71d275b-8f83-5bf3-90f9-5e8ed34d76ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269244 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6b/6be1f51600ae7b42ad0c4c8f67eb8f3ba3570606ec4e3e5e75c65df397aab060.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bfc4c8a3-4cfa-58b3-bd85-7a7059d385d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269245 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186369716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369714 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "12561a6a-dd60-5043-b724-be8966244b1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369715 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369715 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369715 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "843730e5-1d6c-590a-be6f-f5bc76d354a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369716 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369716 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369716 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7d127bbd-7566-53f6-8abe-dfa396d7a93b" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
